# SECTION 1 - FUNCTIONS

A function is a reusable block of code that:
- Takes something in (called parameters)
- Does something to it
- Gives something back (called a return value)

We used four functions that work together in a chain:

Raw text dates
      ↓
clean_dates()           → converts text into real dates Python can calculate
      ↓
calculate_borrow_days() → subtracts checkout from return = number of days
      ↓
flag_late_returns()     → checks if days > 14 = True/False
      ↓
flag_errors()           → checks if days < 0 = True/False

"The functions work together in a chain — first we convert the 
text dates into real date objects, then we subtract one from the 
other to get the number of days borrowed, and finally we use that 
number to automatically flag any late returns or impossible dates. 
Each function does one specific job and passes the result to the next."


# SECTION 2 - DATE FORMATTING

Dates in the raw CSV were stored as plain text e.g. "20/02/2023"
Python cannot do maths on plain text so we had to convert them.

We used datetime.strptime() to convert text into real date objects:

    %d = day   (e.g. 20)
    %m = month (e.g. 02)
    %Y = year  (e.g. 2023)

So "%d/%m/%Y" reads "20/02/2023" correctly in British date format.

"20/02/2023"        ← text string, Python cannot calculate with this
      ↓
datetime.strptime() ← converts it into a real date object
      ↓
returned - checkout ← subtracts the two dates
      ↓
.days               ← extracts just the number e.g. 5
      ↓
days > 14           ← checks if over 14, returns True or False


# SECTION 3 - TIMEDELTA

When you subtract two dates in Python the result is called 
a timedelta object — it represents a duration of time.

Example:
    checkout = 20/02/2023
    returned = 25/02/2023

    returned - checkout = 5 days  ← this is a timedelta object
    .days                          ← extracts just the number 5

We always add .days at the end to get a plain number we can 
compare and work with.


# SECTION 4 - IS_LATE FUNCTION


def is_late(self, checkout_date, return_date, allowed_days=14):
    days = self.calculate_borrow_days(checkout_date, return_date)
    return days > allowed_days

Breaking this down:

- allowed_days=14
  This is a default value. If you do not pass in a number it 
  automatically uses 14 (2 weeks). You could override it like:
  is_late("01/04/2023", "20/04/2023", allowed_days=7) for 1 week.

- self.calculate_borrow_days(...)
  This calls the previous function to get the number of days.

- return days > allowed_days
  This returns True or False. Example:
  days = 19, allowed_days = 14
  19 > 14 = True  ← book is late


